Their findings reveal that wage gains upon migration capture about 38% of the GDP per worker gap between poor countries and the U.S., implying that a significant remaining portion (62%) can be attributed to human capital differences. These results suggest that human capital accounts for a larger share of income differences than previously thought, contradicting earlier literature that underestimated this factor due to selection biases.

The study also considers imperfect skill transferability and selection effects. It shows that less-educated immigrants benefit more from migration in terms of wage gains due to imperfect substitution between unskilled and skilled labor. This supports their estimate of the human capital share in development accounting being between one-half to two-thirds, positing a point estimate of 60%.

The authors address selection issues by comparing characteristics of immigrants to non-migrants, finding that immigrants, particularly from poorer countries, are highly selected based on education and wages. Such selection biases in existing literature previously skewed results against the role of human capital.

